# Cursor Artifact Conventions

This rule governs how new rules, commands, and subagents are created for this project. Always apply it when asked to create or modify any Cursor configuration file.

All artifacts live in `~/dotfiles/cursor/` and are symlinked into each worktree via `.cursor/`. Never create artifacts directly in a worktree — always create them in dotfiles.

---

# Rules (`.cursor/rules/*.mdc`)

## When to Create a New Rule vs. Extend an Existing One
- Extend an existing rule if the new content is directly related to its scope and won't push it over 500 lines
- Create a new rule if the content represents a distinct concern, activation pattern, or audience
- Never duplicate content across rules — if two rules need the same context, reference the other rule via `@rule-name.mdc`

## Activation Type Decision

| Situation | Type | Frontmatter |
|---|---|---|
| Must always be active (core role, environment) | Always Apply | `alwaysApply: true` |
| Relevant to specific file types | Apply to Specific Files | `globs: "**/*.cpp,**/*.h"` |
| Relevant to a domain or task the agent can infer | Apply Intelligently | `alwaysApply: false` + description only |
| Explicitly invoked for a specific workflow | Apply Manually | no globs, no alwaysApply |

Do not combine globs and description in the same rule — pick one activation mechanism.

## Rule File Structure
```
---
description: "Clear statement of when this rule applies and what it governs"
globs: "glob pattern if file-scoped"
alwaysApply: false
---

# Rule Title

Brief one-line summary of purpose.

[Rule content — focused, actionable, under 500 lines]
```

## Rule Content Standards
- Write like concise internal documentation, not a style guide
- Provide concrete examples for non-obvious patterns
- Reference canonical codebase files with `@path/to/file.cpp` instead of copying content
- Include a code example only when the pattern is genuinely non-obvious
- Do not document things the model already knows (standard C++, common tools)
- Do not add instructions for edge cases that rarely apply — add rules only when you observe repeated mistakes

---

# Commands (`.cursor/commands/*.md`)

Commands are plain markdown — no frontmatter. The filename becomes the slash command name.

## When to Create a Command vs. a Rule
- Use a **command** for a repeatable workflow you invoke explicitly (review, plan, explain)
- Use a **rule** for persistent context or behavior that should be available automatically
- Do not create a command that simply re-states a rule — commands should orchestrate workflows, not duplicate rule content

## Command File Structure
```markdown
# Command Title

One sentence describing what this command does.

## Instructions

Clear, direct instructions for the agent. Reference relevant rules with @rule-name.mdc where applicable.

## [Optional sections as needed — Setup, Output Format, etc.]

[Placeholder for user input if needed, clearly marked]
```

## Command Content Standards
- Start with the context the agent needs before the instructions
- Reference rules with `@` rather than repeating their content
- Include a clearly marked placeholder if the command expects user-provided input
- Define an explicit output format if the command produces structured output

---

# Subagents (`.cursor/agents/*.md`)

## When to Create a Subagent vs. a Command
- Use a **subagent** when the task benefits from running in the background, in parallel, or with isolated context
- Use a **command** when the task is single-purpose and sequential
- Do not create a subagent for something a command handles adequately

## Frontmatter Fields

| Field | Usage |
|---|---|
| `name` | lowercase-with-hyphens, becomes the `/name` invocation |
| `description` | when the agent should delegate to this subagent — be specific |
| `model` | `fast` for mechanical tasks (build, test, verify), `inherit` for reasoning tasks |
| `is_background` | `true` only if the task genuinely benefits from running while other work continues |

## Subagent File Structure
```markdown
---
name: subagent-name
description: Specific description of when to use this subagent.
model: fast | inherit
is_background: true | false
---

One sentence role statement.

# [Domain-specific context if needed]

# Process

Numbered steps the subagent follows.

# Output Format

Explicit structure for what the subagent reports back.

# When to Stop

Conditions under which the subagent stops and surfaces a blocker rather than proceeding.
```

## Subagent Content Standards
- Always include a "When to Stop" section — subagents must know when to escalate rather than improvise
- Always include `direnv exec .` prefix for any build or test commands
- Keep prompts concise — long prompts dilute focus, not add capability
- Include HFT-specific considerations (latency, risk, replay) only when directly relevant to the subagent's task

---

# General Standards Across All Artifacts

- **Version control everything** — all artifacts live in `~/dotfiles/cursor/` and are committed
- **Update when the codebase changes** — when an architectural migration completes, update relevant rules to reflect the new baseline
- **Prefer updating over creating** — before creating a new artifact, check if an existing one should be extended
- **Test by using** — after creating a new artifact, use it in a real session and refine based on observed behavior
